__d(function(a,e,t,i,n,s,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:9,chapter_title:"The Living Organisms and Their Surroundings",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Define habitat and identify the habitat of common animals and plants","Distinguish between biotic and abiotic components of a habitat","Explain what adaptation means and why it is important for survival","Describe adaptations of organisms in desert, aquatic, mountain, and grassland habitats","List the seven characteristics common to all living organisms","Differentiate between terrestrial and aquatic habitats with examples","Recognise the role of decomposers in the ecosystem","Appreciate Karnataka's biodiversity, especially the Western Ghats"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic knowledge that animals live in different environments","Awareness that plants grow in different conditions (some need lots of water, others don't)","Understanding from previous chapters about food chains (herbivores, carnivores)","Familiarity with common Karnataka animals and plants from daily life"],introduction:{why_important:"Understanding habitats and adaptations explains why lions cannot survive in the ocean, why cacti don't grow in ponds, and why fish die on land. It reveals nature's elegant engineering \u2014 every organism is perfectly designed for where it lives. This chapter builds the foundation for ecology, evolution, and environmental science.",real_life_relevance:"When you visit Bannerghatta National Park near Bengaluru, or the Kabini wildlife sanctuary, you see animals perfectly matched to their forest habitat. When you travel from Bengaluru to the coast and see coconut trees instead of eucalyptus, you are seeing habitat-driven plant distribution. Adaptation is visible everywhere around you.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka is ecologically one of India's most diverse states. The Western Ghats \u2014 running through Kodagu, Hassan, Chikkamagaluru, Dakshina Kannada, and Uttara Kannada districts \u2014 is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the world's 36 biodiversity hotspots. It is home to tigers, leopards, Asian elephants, lion-tailed macaques, and thousands of endemic plant species. The Deccan Plateau occupies central Karnataka with grassland and scrub habitats. The coastal strip hosts mangrove habitats along the Kali, Sharavathi, and Netravathi rivers. Karnataka's varied topography \u2014 coast, ghats, plateau, and semi-arid zones \u2014 creates multiple distinct habitats, making it ideal for studying habitat and adaptation.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 8 covered body movements and the skeletal system. This chapter extends that by asking: why do animals have the body structures they do? The answer lies in their habitat and the adaptations that evolved for it."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"The Camel",role:"The desert specialist \u2014 demonstrates how structural adaptations (hump, padded feet, long eyelashes) allow survival in extreme arid conditions"},{name:"The Fish",role:"The aquatic expert \u2014 demonstrates adaptations for underwater life (gills, fins, streamlined body, scales)"},{name:"The Cactus",role:"The desert plant \u2014 shows how plant adaptations (spines, thick stem, waxy coat) conserve water"},{name:"The Lotus",role:"Karnataka's state flower and a model hydrophyte \u2014 shows aquatic plant adaptations (floating leaves, aerenchyma, flexible stem)"},{name:"Decomposers",role:"The invisible heroes \u2014 bacteria and fungi that recycle nutrients and maintain soil fertility"}],plot:["The chapter opens by asking why different animals live in different places \u2014 why do fish live in water but birds live on land?","It introduces the concept of habitat \u2014 the natural home of an organism \u2014 and biotic and abiotic components.","It presents the seven characteristics of all living organisms (MRS GREN) to distinguish living from non-living.","It explores adaptations in desert habitats using the camel and cactus as case studies.","It examines aquatic adaptations in fish, frogs, and water plants like lotus and hydrilla.","It discusses adaptations in mountain habitats \u2014 conical trees and thick-furred animals.","It covers grassland adaptations \u2014 camouflage, speed, cooperative hunting.","It explains the role of decomposers in completing the nutrient cycle.","It concludes by connecting habitat conservation to preserving biodiversity, with a focus on Karnataka's Western Ghats."],moral:"Every living organism is exquisitely adapted to its habitat through millions of years of evolution. Destroying habitats means destroying the finely tuned lives of countless organisms \u2014 and ultimately threatens our own survival."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Habitat and Its Components",simple_explanation:"A habitat is the natural home of an organism. It has living parts (biotic) and non-living parts (abiotic). Both work together to support life.",detailed_explanation:"A habitat is the natural environment where an organism lives, finds food, reproduces, and interacts with others. Every habitat has biotic components (all living things \u2014 plants, animals, fungi, bacteria) and abiotic components (all non-living factors \u2014 sunlight, temperature, water, soil, minerals, air, topography). The interaction between biotic and abiotic components creates the unique conditions of each habitat. Major habitat types: terrestrial (land \u2014 forests, deserts, grasslands, mountains), aquatic (water \u2014 ponds, rivers, lakes, oceans), and transitional (wetlands, mangroves). A single organism can also influence its habitat \u2014 earthworms improve soil structure; trees create shade and humidity.",real_life_example:"A pond near your school is a complete habitat. Its biotic components include fish, frogs, water plants, insects, and bacteria. Its abiotic components include the water itself, sunlight reaching the surface, dissolved oxygen, temperature, and the mud at the bottom. Remove any of these and the pond habitat changes drastically.",karnataka_example:"The Ranganathittu Bird Sanctuary (Mandya district) is an aquatic habitat on the Kaveri river where over 200 bird species nest. Its biotic components include painted storks, open-billed storks, egrets, cormorants, mugger crocodiles, and river vegetation. Abiotic components include the flowing river water, sunlight, rocky islands, and seasonal water levels. This habitat is protected by law because it supports rare and migratory birds.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse habitat with ecosystem. A habitat is where an organism lives; an ecosystem includes all organisms and their interactions with the environment in a given area. Also, students sometimes list only animals as biotic components \u2014 plants, fungi, and bacteria are equally important biotic components.",memory_tip:"Habitat = HOME. Biotic = living things in the home (family). Abiotic = non-living parts of the home (walls, sunlight, water supply). Both make the home liveable."},{concept_name:"Characteristics of Living Organisms",simple_explanation:"All living things \u2014 from a tiny bacterium to a blue whale \u2014 share seven common characteristics. These features separate the living from the non-living.",detailed_explanation:"The seven characteristics of living organisms (MRS GREN): (1) Movement \u2014 organisms can move (animals move their whole body; plants move parts like leaves toward sunlight); (2) Respiration \u2014 organisms release energy from food through chemical reactions; (3) Sensitivity \u2014 organisms respond to stimuli (light, heat, touch, sound); (4) Growth \u2014 organisms increase in size and complexity over time; (5) Reproduction \u2014 organisms produce offspring; (6) Excretion \u2014 organisms remove metabolic waste from their bodies; (7) Nutrition \u2014 organisms obtain food for energy and growth. A non-living object like fire seems to grow, move, and 'breathe' \u2014 but it cannot reproduce or excrete. All seven characteristics must be present for something to be classified as living.",real_life_example:"A sunflower plant shows all seven: it moves (tracks sunlight \u2014 heliotropism), respires, is sensitive (closes flowers at night), grows (from seed to tall plant), reproduces (produces seeds), excretes waste through leaves, and obtains nutrition through photosynthesis. Even a single-celled amoeba shows all seven characteristics.",karnataka_example:"The Indian elephant in Nagarhole National Park, Karnataka, shows all seven characteristics dramatically: it moves vast distances (migration); respires continuously; responds to threats (runs from fire, recognises humans); grows from a 100 kg calf to a 5,000 kg adult; reproduces (calves born every 4-6 years); excretes (dung is vital for seed dispersal in the forest); and obtains nutrition (eats 150 kg of vegetation daily).",common_mistakes:"Students often think fire is alive because it seems to grow, move, and need oxygen. But fire cannot reproduce, excrete, or obtain nutrition like a living organism \u2014 it fails the full MRS GREN test. Also, some students forget that plants also move and respond to stimuli, just more slowly than animals.",memory_tip:"MRS GREN: Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity, Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition. Imagine a living Mrs Gren who does all seven things every day. Non-living things cannot do all seven."},{concept_name:"Adaptation",simple_explanation:"Adaptation is any feature of an organism \u2014 physical or behavioural \u2014 that helps it survive and reproduce in its specific habitat.",detailed_explanation:"Adaptations are the result of millions of years of evolution. They can be: (1) Structural \u2014 physical features of the body (camel's hump, fish's gills, polar bear's thick fur, cactus spines); (2) Behavioural \u2014 actions or habits (bird migration, hibernation, nocturnal activity, group hunting by lions); (3) Physiological \u2014 internal body processes (camel's ability to tolerate high body temperature, kangaroo rat's metabolic water production). Adaptations are specific to the habitat \u2014 a fish's gills are perfect in water but useless on land. If a habitat changes faster than organisms can adapt, those organisms go extinct. This is why habitat destruction is so dangerous for biodiversity.",real_life_example:"Compare your hand to a bat's wing \u2014 they have the same bones (five finger-like structures) but adapted for completely different purposes. The bat's 'hand' has thin skin stretched between elongated fingers to form a wing. This is a structural adaptation for flight. Your hand is adapted for gripping and manipulating objects. Same evolutionary origin, different adaptations.",karnataka_example:"The lion-tailed macaque (Sinhakesari in Kannada), found only in the Western Ghats including Karnataka's Silent Valley and Kudremukh forests, has a distinctive silver mane and black body \u2014 adapted for life in dense rainforest canopy. Its long tail provides balance on high branches; forward-facing eyes give depth perception for jumping between trees; strong jaw muscles crack hard forest fruits. These are adaptations specific to the Western Ghats rainforest habitat. It is critically endangered due to habitat loss.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes confuse individual learning with adaptation. If a person learns to swim, that is not an adaptation \u2014 adaptations are inherited (passed from parent to offspring). Also, not all physical features are adaptations \u2014 some are neutral or even vestigial (like the human appendix). Adaptation means the feature specifically helps survival or reproduction in that habitat.",memory_tip:"ADAPT = Animals Design A Perfect Toolkit. Every adaptation is a tool in the toolkit for surviving that specific habitat. The desert toolkit \u2260 the ocean toolkit."},{concept_name:"Desert and Aquatic Adaptations",simple_explanation:"Desert organisms conserve water and tolerate heat. Aquatic organisms are built for life in water \u2014 breathing dissolved oxygen and moving efficiently through water.",detailed_explanation:"Desert adaptations (for water conservation and heat tolerance): Camel \u2014 hump (fat store), padded feet (sand), closeable nostrils, long eyelashes, concentrated urine. Cactus \u2014 thick fleshy stem (water storage), spines instead of leaves (minimises surface area and water loss), waxy stem coating (prevents evaporation), stomata open only at night (CAM photosynthesis). Kangaroo rat \u2014 nocturnal (active only at night), metabolic water from seeds, concentrated urine. Aquatic adaptations (for breathing, moving, and living in water): Fish \u2014 streamlined body, gills (oxygen extraction), fins (propulsion and steering), lateral line (vibration detection), swim bladder (buoyancy). Hydrophytes (lotus, hydrilla) \u2014 aerenchyma (air spaces for buoyancy and oxygen), floating or submerged leaves, flexible stems. Frogs \u2014 moist skin for gas exchange in water, webbed feet for swimming, strong hind legs for jumping on land.",real_life_example:"Compare how much water you drink in summer vs. winter. Now imagine living in the Thar Desert \u2014 you'd die of dehydration in days without special adaptations. The camel solves this with a suite of adaptations that work together as a system. No single adaptation is enough \u2014 it's the combination that allows desert survival.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's Deccan Plateau scrubland habitats in Kalaburagi, Raichur, and Vijayapura districts support drought-adapted plants like Prosopis (babul), Euphorbia (spurge \u2014 a succulent), and various thorny shrubs. These plants have small, waxy leaves or no leaves (photosynthesis through green stems), deep roots, and thorns for protection \u2014 classic xerophyte adaptations. The blackbuck antelope in Ranebennur Blackbuck Sanctuary, Karnataka, is adapted for open grassland \u2014 its slender body reduces heat absorption and it can run at 80 km/h to escape predators.",common_mistakes:"Students often state that a camel's hump stores water \u2014 this is incorrect. The hump stores fat. Also, students may think aquatic animals can breathe oxygen dissolved in water using lungs \u2014 fish breathe dissolved oxygen through gills, not lungs. Whales and dolphins are aquatic but breathe air through lungs \u2014 they must surface regularly.",memory_tip:"Desert organisms are WATER MISERS: store it, save it, make it. Aquatic organisms are WATER LOVERS: built for it, breathe in it, move through it. The adaptations point in opposite directions for opposite problems."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Habitat and Its Components",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name two biotic and two abiotic components of a forest habitat.",a:"Biotic: trees (plants), deer (animals). Abiotic: sunlight, rainfall (also accept: soil, temperature, air)."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why are both biotic and abiotic components necessary for a habitat?",a:"Biotic components (organisms) depend on abiotic factors (sunlight, water, soil) for food and energy. Remove sunlight and plants die; remove plants and animals starve. Both are essential for the habitat to function."},{type:"Application",q:"List the habitat and one adaptation of: (a) a polar bear (b) a cactus.",a:"(a) Polar bear \u2014 polar/Arctic habitat; adaptation: thick white fur for insulation and camouflage. (b) Cactus \u2014 desert habitat; adaptation: spines instead of leaves to reduce water loss."}]}],key_takeaways:["A habitat is the natural environment where an organism lives, finds food, and reproduces.","Habitats have biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components that interact.","All living organisms share seven characteristics: MRS GREN (Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity, Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition).","Adaptation is any feature (structural or behavioural) that helps an organism survive in its habitat.","Desert adaptations conserve water and tolerate heat \u2014 camels, cacti, kangaroo rats.","Aquatic adaptations allow life in water \u2014 gills, fins, streamlined body (fish); aerenchyma, floating leaves (hydrophytes).","Mountain adaptations deal with cold and snow \u2014 thick fur, conical tree shape, needle leaves.","Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) recycle nutrients by breaking down dead matter \u2014 vital for soil fertility.","The Western Ghats (Karnataka) is a global biodiversity hotspot with thousands of endemic species.","Habitat destruction threatens organisms whose adaptations are specific to that habitat \u2014 they cannot simply 'move' elsewhere."],mind_map:{root:"The Living Organisms and Their Surroundings",branches:[{branch:"Habitat",sub:["Biotic components \u2014 plants, animals, fungi, bacteria","Abiotic components \u2014 sunlight, water, soil, temperature","Types: Terrestrial, Aquatic, Amphibious, Arboreal"]},{branch:"Characteristics of Living Things",sub:["MRS GREN","Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity","Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition"]},{branch:"Adaptation",sub:["Structural \u2014 body features (gills, hump, spines)","Behavioural \u2014 actions (migration, hibernation)","Physiological \u2014 internal processes (metabolic water)"]},{branch:"Desert Habitat",sub:["Camel \u2014 hump (fat), padded feet, closeable nostrils","Cactus \u2014 spines, thick stem, waxy coating, night stomata","Kangaroo rat \u2014 nocturnal, metabolic water"]},{branch:"Aquatic Habitat",sub:["Fish \u2014 gills, fins, streamlined body, swim bladder","Lotus \u2014 aerenchyma, waxy floating leaves","Frog \u2014 moist skin, webbed feet"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Western Ghats \u2014 biodiversity hotspot","Ranganathittu \u2014 aquatic bird sanctuary","Nagarhole \u2014 forest habitat for elephants, tigers","Ranebennur \u2014 blackbuck grassland habitat"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 9 introduces habitat (natural home of an organism with biotic and abiotic components) and adaptation (features helping survival). All living organisms share MRS GREN characteristics: Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity, Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition. Desert adaptations focus on water conservation: camel (fat hump, padded feet, closeable nostrils, concentrated urine), cactus (spines not leaves, thick water-storing stem, waxy coating, night-opening stomata), kangaroo rat (metabolic water, nocturnal). Aquatic adaptations: fish (gills, fins, streamlined body, swim bladder, lateral line), hydrophytes like lotus (aerenchyma, floating waxy leaves, flexible stem), frog (webbed feet, moist skin). Mountain adaptations: conical tree shape, needle leaves, thick fur. Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) recycle nutrients \u2014 essential for nutrient cycle. Karnataka connections: Western Ghats (biodiversity hotspot), Ranganathittu (bird sanctuary/aquatic habitat), Nagarhole and Bannerghatta (forest habitats), Ranebennur Blackbuck Sanctuary (grassland). Key Karnataka-specific organism: lion-tailed macaque (endemic to Western Ghats).",must_remember_facts:["Habitat = the natural home of an organism, with biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components.","Biotic = living things. Abiotic = non-living factors (sunlight, water, soil, temperature).","MRS GREN = Movement, Respiration, Sensitivity, Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition \u2014 characteristics of all living organisms.","Adaptation = inherited features (structural or behavioural) that help an organism survive in its habitat.","Camel's hump stores FAT, not water. Water is stored in the bloodstream and body tissues.","Cactus leaves are modified into SPINES to reduce water loss; the thick green stem does photosynthesis.","Fish use GILLS to extract dissolved oxygen from water; they cannot breathe air (unlike whales and dolphins).","Lotus is Karnataka's state flower and a hydrophyte with aerenchyma (air spaces) for buoyancy.","Decomposers (bacteria and fungi) break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil.","The Western Ghats (through Kodagu, Hassan, Chikkamagaluru districts of Karnataka) is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and global biodiversity hotspot."]}},2533,[]);